Q: how to increase the height dynamically of 2nd div on giving more data in 1st Div A: <p>The problem is that <code>position: absolute</code> removes the elements from the normal document flow, so the parent and <code>Div1</code> won't adjust when <code>Div2</code> grows.</p>
<p>You can let the elements stay in the normal flow instead:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div id=&quot;Div2&quot;&gt;
        Your dynamic content here...
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div id=&quot;Div1&quot;&gt;
        Your second div content
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>.container {
    width: 391px;
}

#Div2,
#Div1 {
    width: 87px;
}

#Div1 {
    margin-top: 10px;
}
</code></pre>
<p>With this approach, when <code>Div2</code> gets more content, its height grows automatically, <code>Div1</code> moves down, and the parent container grows with it. No JavaScript is required.</p>

Q: Limit Google Sheet to Only Be Viewed on a Tuesday (preferably between certain hours) A: <p>The <code>manageTimeAccess()</code> function is embedded inside <code>myFunction()</code> which makes it unreachable from many kinds of call methods, such as <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/menus#clickable_images_and_drawings_in_google_sheets" rel="nofollow noreferrer">buttons</a>, <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/menus" rel="nofollow noreferrer">custom menu items</a> or <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/triggers/installable" rel="nofollow noreferrer">installable triggers</a>.</p>
<p>To make the function testable, unwrap the <code>manageTimeAccess()</code> function by removing the initial <code>function myFunction() {</code> line and the trailing <code>}</code> at the last line, like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function manageTimeAccess() {
  const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActive();
  const timezone = ss.getSpreadsheetTimeZone();
  const get_ = format =&gt; Utilities.formatDate(new Date(), timezone, format);
  const weekday = get_('EEE');
  const time = get_('hh:mm');
  const file = DriveApp.getFileById(ss.getId());
  const access = file.getSharingAccess();
  if (weekday === 'Tue' &amp;&amp; '09:40' &lt;= time &amp;&amp; time &lt;= '15:00' &amp;&amp; access !== DriveApp.Access.ANYONE_WITH_LINK) {
    file.setSharing(DriveApp.Access.ANYONE_WITH_LINK, DriveApp.Permission.VIEW);
    console.log('Access OPENED for viewers.');
  } else if (access === DriveApp.Access.ANYONE_WITH_LINK) {
    file.setSharing(DriveApp.Access.PRIVATE, DriveApp.Permission.EDIT);
    console.log('Access LOCKED for viewers.');
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>To run the code repeatedly, manually add a <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/triggers/installable" rel="nofollow noreferrer">time-driven trigger</a> to execute it every five minutes.</p>
<p>Note that you'll have to test the code yourself. See <a href="https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/support/troubleshooting#debugging" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apps Script debugging</a>, <a href="https://ericlippert.com/2014/03/05/how-to-debug-small-programs/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">How to debug small programs</a>, <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/tags/google-apps-script/info">Apps Script at Stack Overflow</a> and <a href="https://github.com/ryanmcdermott/clean-code-javascript" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Clean Code JavaScript</a>.</p>

